   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_t6gjPHHYsyd9BcNj",
   "name": "Resistors in series & parallel",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "860091344"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Resistors in series & parallel"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Combination of resistance in series"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When two or more components are connected in **series**:\n\n  - The **combined **resistance of the components is equal to the** sum **of individual resistances"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/02/combined-resistance.png",
   "file": "assets/3aedd078ebf8-combined-resistance.png",
   "alt": "combined-resistance, IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***When several components are connected in series, their combined resistance is equal to the sum of their individual resistances***"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Combination of resistance in parallel"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When resistors are connected in parallel, the **combined **resistance is **less than **the resistance of any of the individual components\n- If two resistors of equal resistance are connected in parallel, then the combined resistance will halve"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/05/Equal-resistors-parallel.png",
   "file": "assets/d303fa80286a-equal-resistors-parallel.png",
   "alt": "Equal resistors parallel, IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***The above resistors will have a combined resistance of 2 Ω − half the value of each resistor in this case***"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_YhP4q9dSJKsW4z7y",
   "name": "Determining resistance in parallel",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Determining resistance in parallel"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Extended Tier Only"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- To determine the combined resistance of any combination of two resistors, you must use the equation:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\frac{1}{R}=\\frac{1}{R_{1}}+\\frac{1}{R_{2}}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $R$ is the combined resistance in ohms, Ω\n  - $R_{1}$ is the resistance of resistor 1 in ohms, Ω\n  - $R_{2}$ is the resistance of resistor 2 in ohms, Ω"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/02/resistors-parallel.png",
   "file": "assets/e0bda0f72dba-resistors-parallel.png",
   "alt": "resistors-parallel, IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/06/Resistors-in-parallel-equation.png",
   "file": "assets/ed8311722d9f-resistors-in-parallel-equation.png",
   "alt": "Resistors in parallel equation, IGCSE & GCSE Physics revision notes",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***The combined resistance is always less than the resistance of either resistor individually***"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The method to calculate the resistance:\n\n  - First find the value of *1/R* (by adding *1/R*<sub>*1*</sub>*+ 1/R*<sub>*2*</sub>)\n  - Next find the value of *R* by using the reciprocal button on your calculator (labelled either *x*<sup>*-1*</sup>* *or *1/x*, depending on your calculator)"
  },
